# Structure-induced hollow Co3O4 nanoparticles with rich oxygen vacancies for efficient CO oxidation; 结构诱导富集氧空位的空心Co3O4催化CO高效氧化;

## Structure-induced hollow $\cos_{3}0_{4}$ nanoparticles with rich oxygen vacancies for efficient CO oxidation
Zhijie Chen, Yajing Wang, Qiannan Liang, Liyu Chen, Weiteng Zhan and Yingwei Li
ABSTRACT $\mathbf{Co_{3}O_{4}}$ has been considered as one kind of promising catalysts for the oxidation of CO. According to the Mars-van Krevelen mechanism, oxygen vacancies of $\mathbf{Co_{3}O_{4}}$ play a significant role in catalytic activity. Herein, we report a novel structure-induced strategy to develop hollow $\mathbf{Co_{3}O_{4}}$ with rich oxygen vacancies for efficient oxidation of CO. Through a reduction-oxidation pyrolysis process, the metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) precursor (i.e., ZIF-67) is transformed into $\mathbf{H}{\cdot}\mathbf{Co}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}@\mathbf{H}{\cdot}\mathbf{C},$ in which hollow $\mathbf{Co_{3}O_{4}}$ $\left(\mathbf{H}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{Co}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}\right)$ nanoparticles $(\mathbf{NPs})$ are embedded in hollow carbon (H-C) shell. The hollow $\mathbf{Co_{3}O_{4}}$ NPs feature rich oxygen vacancies and finish a complete conversion of CO at $\bf{130^{\circ}C,}$ which is much lower than that of solid $\mathbf{Co_{3}O_{4}}$ (the temperature of full CO conversion $\scriptstyle T_{100}=220^{\circ}C,$ ). Besides, the hollow carbon shell could also reduce the diffusion resistance during the oxidation process. Benefiting from the unique hollow structures, $\mathbf{H}{-}\mathbf{Co}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}@\mathbf{H}{-}\mathbf{C}$ even shows comparable activity to noble metal catalysts under high weight hourly space velocities (WHSVs) up to $240,000\mathrm{mLh}^{-1}\mathrm{g_{\mathrm{cat.}}}^{-1}$ . Furthermore, the $\mathbf{H}{-}\mathbf{Co}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}@\mathbf{H}{-}\mathbf{C}$ catalyst also shows good durability with only a slight decline after the reaction has been operated for $24\mathbf{h}$ .
Keywords: CO oxidation, metal-organic frameworks, cobalt oxide, hollow structure, oxygen vacancy
## INTRODUCTION
With the development of modern industry, oxidation of carbon monoxide is increasingly important in relation to in-door air quality control and automotive emissions purification. Tricobalt tetraoxide $(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4})$ has been demonstrated to be an efficient catalyst for the oxidation of CO [1–4]. It is generally accepted that the CO oxidation catalyzed by $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ proceeds via the Mars-van Krevelen mechanism, in which the oxygen vacancies on the surface of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ can activate $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ molecules to form actively adsorbed oxygen $\left(\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{ads}}\right)$ that would react with CO adsorbed on ${\mathrm{Co}}^{3+}$ ions to produce $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ [5–7]. The initial oxygen vacancies determine the amount of $\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{ads}}$ on the surface of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ to affect the activity of CO oxidation [8–10]. In this regard, it is desirable to effectively enrich the oxygen vacancies on the surface of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ .
The traditional way to create oxygen vacancies on the surface of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ is to calcine $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ at high temperature [11]. During the calcination process, some oxygen species on the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ surface would release to form oxygen vacancies. However, the treatments at high temperatures would inevitably result in severe agglomeration of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ . Besides, it has been reported that the surface atomic configuration of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ was strongly influenced by its morphology [12]. Specially, metal oxides with hollow structures were demonstrated to possess more defect sites on their surfaces than the solid counterparts [13–18]. Therefore, we could reasonably speculate that fabrication of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ with hollow structure may maximize the oxygen vacancies on its surface to achieve high catalytic efficiency in CO oxidation.
Recently, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), constructed by inorganic nodes with organic linkers, have been used as versatile templates for preparing a variety of functional materials, including metal oxides [19–27]. Through controlling the pyrolysis atmosphere and temperatures, metal oxides with different morphologies could be synthesized [28–31], providing great opportunity to tune their exposed surface configurations with enriched oxygen vacancies. In this work, we report a novel structure-induced strategy to enrich the surface oxygen vacancies through fabricating $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ NPs with hollow structure derived from MOFs. As a proof of concept, we selected a Co-based MOF (i.e., ZIF-67) as the precursor for the synthesis of hollow $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ NPs embedded in hollow carbon shell (denoted as $\mathrm{H-Co_{3}O_{4}@H-C)}$ via a reduction-oxidation pyrolysis process. The hollow $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ structure possessed rich oxygen vacancies on the surface for the activation of $\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{:}$ and the hollow carbon shell enhanced the substrate diffusion and also stabilized the hollow $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ NPs during the oxidation process. The resulting $H{-}\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{H}{-}\mathrm{C}$ composites showed excellent catalytic activity and durability for CO oxidation, achieving a complete CO conversion at $130^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and a high weight hourly space velocity (WHSV) of $60,000\mathrm{mLh}^{-1}\mathbf{g}_{\mathrm{cat.}}^{-1}$ for up to $24\mathrm{h}$ of reaction time, representing a highly efficient catalyst for CO oxidation.
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
All chemicals used in this work were purchased from commercial sources (Sigma-Aldrich, Alfa Aesar, and others) and used without further purification.
## Materials synthesis
In a typical synthesis of ZIF-67, cobalt nitrate hexahydrate $\mathrm{875.4\mg},$ 3 mmol) and 2-methylimidazole 1 $(990~\mathrm{mg}.$ , $12\mathrm{mmol}$ ) were dissolved into $75~\mathrm{mL}$ of methanol, respectively. Then the two solutions were quickly mixed. After being stirred for a few seconds, the mixed solution was left for aging for $24\mathrm{h}$ at room temperature. The resulting purple precipitates were collected by centrifugation, subsequently washed with methanol for 3 times, and finally dried under vacuum at $80^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ .
For comparison purpose, another two samples were prepared by using ZIF-67 as precursors. The synthesis of $\mathrm{H-Co_{3}O_{4}}@\mathrm{C}$ was the same as that of $H{-}\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{H}{-}\mathrm{C}$ except that the $\mathrm{Ar/H}_{2}$ was replaced by pure Ar at the beginning. $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{H}\mathrm{-C}$ was prepared according to the previous report with minor modifications [32]. $0.5\mathrm{\g}$ of ZIF-67 was placed in a tubular furnace and then heated at $450^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ with a heating rate of $2^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ under air. After being cooled down to room temperature, the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{H}\mathrm{-C}$ material was obtained.
$\mathrm{Co}@\mathrm{C}{\cdot}\mathrm{Ar}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ or $\operatorname{Co}@\operatorname{C-Ar}$ was obtained directly by calcinating ZIF-67 under an $\mathrm{Ar/H}_{2}$ or Ar atmosphere at $600^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{3\mathrm{h}}$ , respectively.
## Materials characterization
## Catalytic tests
The catalytic activities of the samples for CO oxidation were measured in a fixed bed micro-reactor $(9\mathrm{mm}$ i.d.) under ambient pressure. Typically, $50~\mathrm{mg}$ of the catalyst was loaded into the reactor and pretreated in $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ at $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ to remove moisture and adsorbed impurities. After being cooled to room temperature, a gas mixture containing $1\mathrm{\vol\%}$ CO and $99\mathrm{vol}\%$ air were introduced into the reactors at a flow rate of $50\mathrm{mL}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ using mass flow controllers, corresponding to a WHSV of $60,000\mathrm{mLg_{cat.}}^{-1}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ . The composition of the effluent gases was monitored using an online gas chromatograph equipped with a thermal conductivity detector (TCD). The catalytic data were collected after $30~\mathrm{min}$ for each temperature to ensure a steady-state condition. For the stability test, the reactions were conducted under the same reaction conditions as described above.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Scheme 1 illustrated the synthetic routes for preparing cobalt-based materials using ZIF-67 as template. Among these MOF-derived materials, $H{-}\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{H}{-}\mathrm{C}$ was prepared through a reduction-oxidation pyrolysis process. ZIF-67 was first treated in $\mathrm{Ar/H}_{2}$ at $600^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . During this step, the cobalt cations in the ZIF-67 frameworks were reduced into Co NPs, as was evident from the XRD pattern of $\mathrm{Co}@\mathrm{C}{\cdot}\mathrm{Ar}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ (Fig. S1). Then, air was introduced as oxidant to implement the succedent oxidation at $350^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to yield $H{-}\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{H}{-}\mathrm{C}$ . From the XRD pattern of $\mathrm{H-Co_{3}O_{4}@H-C}$ in Fig. 1a, it was clear to identify that the Co NPs were transformed into $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ NPs within a short time of oxidation.
For comparisons, another two cobalt-based catalysts $\mathrm{(H-Co_{3}O_{4}@C}$ and $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{H}-\mathrm{C})$ were prepared by using different calcination processes. For the synthesis of H$\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{C}$ , a similar preparation method as for $H{-}\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{H}{-}\mathrm{C}$ was employed, only altering the initial atmosphere for $\mathrm{Ar/H}_{2}$ to Ar (Scheme 1). Thus $\operatorname{Co@C-Ar}$ was obtained after the pyrolysis of ZIF-67 under Ar atmosphere and then the followed oxidation converted the Co NPs into $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ NPs inside the final ${\mathrm{H}}–\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{C}$ catalyst (Fig. S1 and Fig. 1a). It is worth noting that the average sizes of Co NPs inside $\mathrm{Co}@\mathrm{C}{\cdot}\mathrm{Ar}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and ${\mathrm{Co}}\textcircled{a}{\mathrm{C}}-$ Ar increased after oxidation as compared with those of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ NPs in $H{-}\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{H}{-}\mathrm{C}$ and ${\mathrm{H}}–\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{C}$ (Table 1), respectively. These results indicated that re-construction might happen to the inner structure of those NPs during the transformation. The $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{H}\mathrm{-C}$ was synthesized directly under air atmosphere at $450^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and the cobalt compounds were assigned to $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ (Fig. 1a).
Although different calcination processes were applied, $\mathrm{H-Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{H-C}.$ ${\mathrm{H-Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}@{\mathrm{C}}$ and $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{H}\mathrm{-C}$ displayed similar porous structures. As shown in Fig. 1b, these cobalt-based materials all displayed low adsorption capacities at low relative pressures, which indicated that the catalysts possessed micropores in their structures. At high relative pressures, all three catalysts exhibited hysteresis loops that could be associated with the existence of mesopores, as evident from the pore distribution curves (Fig. S2) [33–35]. The BET surface areas of the cobaltbased materials are listed in Table 1. Interestingly, the Co content followed the order of $\mathrm{H.Co_{3}O_{4}@C<H.C o_{3}O_{4}@}$ $\mathrm{H-C}<\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{H-C},$ which was opposite to that of their BET surface areas. It probably reflected that ${\mathrm{H}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}@{\mathrm{C}}$ could preserve the porous structure from the template to a greater extent with more loss of the carbon and nitrogen elements resulting in a higher Co content.
In order to investigate the morphology evolution, SEM and TEM were employed to observe the formation of the hollow cavities. After pyrolysis under $\mathrm{Ar/H}_{2},$ the obtained $\mathrm{Co}@\mathrm{C}{\cdot}\mathrm{Ar}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ (Fig. S3d–f) still preserved the rhomboic dodecahedron morphology of the template ZIF-67 (Fig. S3a–c), while generating a hollow cavity with a size of ca. $300~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . It was proposed that, in presence of $\begin{array}{r}{\mathrm{H}_{2},}\end{array}$ the Co species in ZIF-67 was reduced at relative low temperatures and accelerated the pyrolysis process of imidazole ligands and the consumption of carbon [36]. With subsequent introduction of air instead of the reduction gas, the hollow skeleton of $\mathrm{Co@C-Ar/H}_{2}$ was maintained (Fig. 2a, d). Meanwhile, hollow $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ NPs were formed inside the $H{-}\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{H}{-}\mathrm{C}$ material (Fig. 2g). TEM and high-resolution TEM (HRTEM) images of the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\mathrm{NPs}$ in $H{-}\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{H}{-}\mathrm{C}$ (Fig. 3) were taken to observe their structures. It was clear to note that most of the ${\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}{\mathrm{NPs}}$ in Fig. 3a possessed a hollow cavity inside the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ shell, which should be the reason that the average sizes of NPs increased after the oxidation (Table 1). The HRTEM image in Fig. 3b showed a lattice spacing of $0.233~\mathrm{nm}$ , which could be attributed to the (222) plane of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ .
Solid carbon shells were obtained in ${\mathrm{H-Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}@{\mathrm{C}}$ (Fig. 2b, e, h) when the calcination atmosphere was changed to pure Ar, demonstrating that the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ was crucial to fabricate hollow carbon shells. However, hollow $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ NPs were observed in both $H_{-}(\mathrm{-}\mathrm{_{0}}_{3}\mathrm{O_{4}}@\mathrm{H-C}$ and $\mathrm{H-Co_{3}O_{4}}@\mathrm{C},$ which proved that short time of the oxidation process played a significant role. According to the previous reports [37–40], the formation of small cavities in $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ particles was caused by the different diffusion rates during the oxidation of Co NPs. The faster migratory rate of $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Co}$ atoms to the surface as compared with that of $\mathrm{~O~}$ atoms to the core is well-known as the Kirkendall effect. Direct calcination under air atmosphere could only yield a hollow carbon shell with solid $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ NPs, e.g., $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{H}\mathrm{-C}.$ , as shown in Fig. 2c, f, i. The formation mechanism of hollow carbon shell in $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{H}\mathrm{-C}_{\mathrm{-}}$ , which was different from that of ${\mathrm{H}}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@$ H-C, resulting from non-equilibrium heat treatment in which two forces with the opposite direction that led to the interface separation of the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ shell formed at the initial stage and the ZIF-67 core [28]. The high-angle annular dark field (HAADF)-STEM images (Fig. 4) showed that both $H_{-}(\mathrm{\Delta}_{0_{3}}\mathrm{O_{4}}@\mathrm{H}.\mathrm{C}$ and $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{H}\mathrm{-C}$ possessed a hollow carbon shell. Energy dispersive spectrometer (EDS) mapping images of these three catalysts demonstrated that the elements of C, Co, N, and O were all evenly distributed on their bulk particles.
After structural characterizations, the catalytic performances of the as-synthesized $H{-}\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{H}{-}\mathrm{C}$ , $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{H}.$ C, and $\mathrm{H-Co_{3}O_{4}}@\mathrm{C}$ materials in CO oxidation were then investigated to disclose the relationship between their structures and catalytic activities. The reactions were carried out under $1\mathrm{vol}\%$ CO and $99\mathrm{vol}\%$ dry air with a space velocity of $60,000\mathrm{mLh}^{-1}{\bf g}_{\mathrm{cat.}}^{-1}$ . As shown in Fig. 5, using $H{-}\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{H}{-}\mathrm{C}$ as the catalyst, the oxidation process of CO began at $50^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and completed at $130^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (the temperature of full CO conversion, $T_{100})$ . For $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{H}$ - C, $100\%$ conversion of CO was accomplished at $220^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The apparently higher catalytic activity achieved on $H{-}\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{H}{-}\mathrm{C}$ suggested that the hollow cavities in $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ NPs played a significant role in enhancing the activity. Although ${\mathrm{H-Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}@{\mathrm{C}}$ also possessed hollow $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ NPs, the lack of hollow cavity of carbon shell would affect the inner diffusion of the gases, resulting in lower activity ( $\mathrm{\Delta}T_{100}{=}170^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ as compared with $\mathrm{H-Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@$ H-C.
The surface chemical states of Co and O elements can provide important information for the activity of the catalysts for CO oxidation. In general, it is believed that ${\mathrm{Co}}^{3+}$ is essential to the adsorption of CO molecule so that the oxidation could happen with the oxygen atom next to ${\mathrm{Co}}^{3+}$ [41]. For the oxygen element, three kinds of oxygen, i.e., $\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{latt}},$ $\mathrm{\DeltaO_{ads},}$ and physisorbed and chemisorbed water $\mathrm{(O_{wat})}$ , may exist in the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ . Among them, the content of $\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{ads}}$ could reflect the amount of the initial oxygen vacancies on the catalyst surfaces. Besides, the $\mathrm{\DeltaO_{ads}}$ is considered to own higher mobility than the $\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{latt}}$ so that it could react with CO adsorbed on $\mathrm{\dot{C}o}^{3+}$ to generate oxygen vacancies more quickly [42]. Therefore, the balance between the contents of ${\mathrm{Co}}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{\DeltaO_{ads}}$ is essential for the high activity of CO oxidation, as the equilibrium between CO capture and oxygen supplement could reach the highest efficiency.
To figure out the impact of ${\mathrm{Co}}^{3+}$ and initial oxygen vacancies, XPS analysis was used to determine the surface chemical states of the cobalt-based catalysts. As shown in Fig. 6a, the compositions of $H{-}\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{H}{-}\mathrm{C}$ , Hb- $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ $@\mathrm{C},$ and $\mathrm{H-Co_{3}O_{4}}@\mathrm{C}$ were identical with C, N, O and Co as the main elements whose binding energies were 284.48, 399.44, 540.67, and $780.24\:\mathrm{eV}$ , respectively. According to the literature [11], the $\mathrm{Co}2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ peak could be fitted with five peaks that were at 779.85, 780.90, 782.40, 786.85 and $790.10\mathrm{eV}$ , assigning to ${\mathrm{Co}}^{3+}$ , both of ${\mathrm{Co}}^{3+}$ and ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ , ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ , satellite peak 1 (Sat. 1), and satellite peak 2 (Sat. 2), respectively (Fig. 6b). The values of the relative areas of these five peaks are listed in Table S1. $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{H}\mathrm{-C}$ calcined directly under dry air possessed higher ${\mathrm{Co}}^{3+}$ content compared with $H{-}\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{H}{-}\mathrm{C}$ and $\mathrm{H-Co_{3}O_{4}}@\mathrm{C}$ . Taking the catalytic behaviors into consideration, the content of ${\mathrm{Co}}^{3+}$ in these catalysts seemed to be enough to capture CO from the reaction gas, while the oxygen supplement was limited. Therefore, the content of the $\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{ads}}$ was regarded as the key to affect the catalytic activity
of CO oxidation.
In Fig. 6c, the XPS spectra of O 1s were fitted with three types of peaks, including the $\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{latt}}$ at $530.00\mathrm{eV}$ , $\mathrm{\DeltaO_{ads}}$ at $531.40\mathrm{eV}$ , and $\mathrm{\DeltaO_{wat}}$ at $532.80\mathrm{eV}$ . The $\mathrm{{O_{ads}/O_{l a t t}}}$ molar ratios (Table 1) were calculated from their relative area to evaluate the quantity of initial oxygen vacancies on the catalyst surface [10]. As excepted, $H\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{H-C}$ owned the highest value of the $\mathrm{O_{ads}/O_{l a t t}}$ molar ratio among these three catalysts, which was 2.6 times higher than that of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{H}\mathrm{-C}$ . It has been reported that more ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ on $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ surface would result in more oxygen vacancies [8]. Combined with the Co $\mathsf{2p}_{2/3}$ XPS analysis, $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{H}\mathrm{-C}$ showed more ${\mathrm{Co}}^{3+}$ but fewer ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ which caused fewer oxygen vacancies on its surface. The $H{-}\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{H}{-}\mathrm{C}$ catalyst with hollow $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ NPs showed higher $\mathrm{O_{ads}/O_{l a t t}}$ molar ratio than $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{H}\mathrm{-C}$ , which demonstrated that the structure-induced strategy could successfully affect the quantity of initial oxygen vacancies by introducing the hollow structure into $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ NPs.
Apart from XPS analysis, $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ -TPR was employed to study the oxygen species in the cobalt-based materials. Normally, the profile of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ consisted of three peaks which were assigned to the reductions of adsorbed oxygen, $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ to CoO, and $\scriptstyle\mathrm{CoO}$ to $\mathrm{Co}$ , respectively [11,43]. The peaks below $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in Fig. 6d could be recognized as the reaction of the adsorbed oxygen with $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ . Their peak areas were supposed to correspond roughly to the quantity of $\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{ads}}$ . As shown in Fig. 6d, the order of peak intensities was $\mathrm{H.Co_{3}O_{4}@H.C>H.C o_{3}O_{4}@C>C o_{3}O_{4}@}$ H-C, which was consistent with the XPS results (Table S1). Interestingly, it was found that although H$\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{C}$ possessed similar hollow $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ NPs as H$\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{H}\mathrm{-C}.$ it had fewer $\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{ads}}$ than the latter. H$\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{C}$ could be affected by the structure of carbon materials that might cover some oxygen vacancies for adsorbing oxygen species. These results indicated that H${\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}@{\mathrm{H}}-{\mathrm{C}}$ possessed the most oxygen vacancies initially, producing more $\mathrm{\DeltaO_{ads}}$ to activate the CO oxidation cycle.
In view of the significant effect of WHSV on catalytic behaviors, the $H\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{H-C}$ catalyst was tested in CO oxidation at different WHSVs. The values of $T_{100}$ for each reaction were recorded and displayed in Fig. 7a. Generally, the catalytic activity was lowered when the WHSV increased because of the shortened residence time of reaction gas on the surface of the catalyst. For the ${\mathrm{H}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{C}}_{0_{3}}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ $\ @\mathrm{H-C}$ catalyst, the $T_{100}$ value was enhanced from 130 to $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ when the WHSV increased from 60,000 to $240,000\mathrm{mLh}^{-1}\mathbf{g}_{\mathrm{cat.}}^{-1}$ . Up to now, there are few reports achieving high activity for CO oxidation at relatively high WHSVs [44,45]. For example, Yan et al. [42] prepared a $\mathrm{Pt/CeO}_{2}$ catalyst which possessed single atomic $\mathrm{Pt}$ on the $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ support and gave a full conversion of CO at $148^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with a WHSV of $\overline{{2}}00,000\mathrm{mLh}^{-1}\mathbf{g}_{\mathrm{cat.}}^{-1}$ . To our delight, $H{-}\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{H}{-}\mathrm{C}$ showed comparable activity to the $\mathrm{Pt}/$ $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ catalyst that achieved complete CO transformation at $190^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ at a WHSV of $210,000{\mathrm{~\dot{m}L h}}^{-1}{\bf g}_{\mathrm{cat.}}^{-1}$ . Taking the supervisor activity of single atom catalyst into consideration, $H{-}\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{H}{-}\mathrm{C}$ exhibited remarkable catalytic activity that is close to noble-metal catalysts at high WHSVs. Moreover, as shown in Fig. 7b, $H\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{H-C}$ also exhibited excellent durability in the oxidation of CO at $130^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with only a slight decline in activity even after $24\mathrm{h}$ of online reaction.
## CONCLUSIONS
In summary, we have developed a novel structureinduced strategy to boost the quantity of oxygen vacancies of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ materials for efficient oxidation of CO by introducing hollow structures into the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ NPs.
Thus, a hollow $H{-}\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{H}{-}\mathrm{C}$ catalyst is successfully fabricated through reduction-oxidation with ZIF-67 as precursor. $H_{-}(\mathrm{{co}}_{3}\mathrm{{O}}_{4}@\mathrm{{H-C}}$ exhibits remarkable catalytic activity, achieving a complete conversion at $130^{\circ}\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{i}}$ , which is even comparable to that of noble-metal catalysts at high WHSVs. The high activity of $H\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{H-C}$ would originate from the hollow $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ NPs featured rich oxygen vacancies that could produce more $\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{ads}}$ to accelerate the CO oxidation cycle. Besides, the hollow carbon shell in $H{-}\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{H}{-}\mathrm{C}$ can exactly expose its inner surface which would increase the quantity of $\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{ads}}$ . The combination of the two kinds of hollow cavities enables the catalyst to retain its high catalytic activity even at very high WHSVs, and shows great durability during the longtime online reaction. The structure-induced strategy might open up a new avenue to the development of novel porous materials with richened oxygen vacancies for highly efficient and durable oxidation reactions.
